[center][size=6][b][color=#b48cff]COD3X[/color][/b][/size]
[size=4][b][color=#d4d4d8]THE OPENMW LUA FIELD MANUAL[/color][/b][/size]

[size=3][color=#b48cff]CODE · CONTEXT · CONSEQUENCE[/color][/size][/center]

[color=#d4d4d8]Cod3x is a [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/][color=#b48cff]field manual[/color][/url] for writing, understanding, debugging, and optimizing OpenMW Lua.[/color]

[color=#d4d4d8]It began as context-aware Lua Language Server annotations. It has grown into something considerably larger: a complete OpenMW Lua reference companion, beginner curriculum, engineering handbook, cookbook, architecture archive, performance guide, and historical record of the things that worked — and the things that absolutely did not.[/color]

[center][size=5][b][color=#b48cff]Write better OpenMW Lua. Understand why it works.[/color][/b][/size][/center]

[color=#d4d4d8]If you have never written Lua before, Cod3x can take you [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/zero-to-hero/][color=#7ee2a8]from zero to a working mod[/color][/url].[/color]

[color=#d4d4d8]If you already ship OpenMW Lua, use it as a reference, architecture guide, performance manual, and source of production-tested patterns.[/color]

[color=#d4d4d8]And if someone tells you OpenMW Lua is simply slow:[/color]

[center][size=5][b][color=#b48cff]No. Your OpenMW Lua is slow.[/color][/b][/size][/center]

[center][url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/performance/measure-first/][color=#ff9875]Measure it. Find out why.[/color][/url][/center]
[heading][center][color=#b48cff]What Cod3x contains[/color][/center]
[/heading]
[center][b][size=4][url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/zero-to-hero/][color=#7ee2a8]Zero to Hero[/color][/url][/size][/b][/center]

[color=#d4d4d8]Never written Lua? Never made an OpenMW script? Do not know what a language server is? Start here.[/color]

[color=#d4d4d8]Zero to Hero walks through Lua fundamentals, your first OpenMW Lua mod, Cod3x setup, script contexts, communication between scripts, objects and records, state, UI, input, and finally a complete small mod that ties the pieces together.[/color]

[center][size=4][b][url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/reference/][color=#b48cff]API Reference[/color][/url][/b][/size][/center]

[color=#d4d4d8]A human-facing reference companion for OpenMW's Lua APIs, types, records, objects, interfaces, handlers, constants, and script-context restrictions.[/color]

[color=#d4d4d8]The goal is not merely to repeat function signatures. Cod3x connects APIs to mental models, examples, architecture, production usage, and the engine behavior underneath them.[/color]

[center][size=4][b][url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/tooling/luals/][color=#83c7ff]LuaLS Tooling[/color][/url][/b][/size][/center]

[color=#d4d4d8]Cod3x still provides the tooling it originally existed to build: detailed Lua Language Server annotations and an OpenMW-aware context plugin.[/color]

[color=#d4d4d8]Autocomplete, hover documentation, type checking, nilability, OpenMW records and objects, interfaces, enums, and context-aware diagnostics all work directly in your editor.[/color]

[center][size=4][b][url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/good-designs/][color=#f2c66d]Good Designs[/color][/url][/b][/size][/center]

[color=#d4d4d8]Real OpenMW systems are used as design studies rather than reduced to generic programming slogans.[/color]

[color=#d4d4d8]Cod3x documents how abstractions such as [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/good-designs/s3lf-semantic-boundary/][color=#b48cff]S3lf[/color][/url], [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/good-designs/protected-table-genealogy/][color=#b48cff]ProtectedTable[/color][/url], [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/good-designs/image-atlas-domain-concept/][color=#b48cff]ImageAtlas[/color][/url], [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/good-designs/cam-helper-extraction/][color=#b48cff]CamHelper[/color][/url], [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/good-designs/state-machine-genealogy/][color=#b48cff]StateMachine[/color][/url], S3maphore's playlist resolver, bounded actor scheduling, and other systems evolved — including when the best answer was to [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/good-designs/delete-fake-objects/][color=#b48cff]delete an abstraction[/color][/url] instead of making it smarter.[/color]

[center][b][color=#b48cff]Sometimes better code is simpler, not smarter.[/color][/b][/center]

[center][size=4][b][url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/performance/][color=#ff9875]Performance[/color][/url][/b][/size][/center]

[color=#d4d4d8]Profiling, allocation, caching, engine-boundary costs, hot-path design, LuaJIT bytecode, traces, side exits, userdata, garbage collection, benchmarking, [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/performance/pr0f1l3r/][color=#b48cff]Pr0f1l3r[/color][/url], and eventually the runtime itself.[/color]

[color=#d4d4d8]Cod3x does not teach optimization folklore. It asks where the work actually happens. Start with [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/performance/measure-first/][color=#ff9875]Measure First[/color][/url], then keep digging into [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/performance/engine-boundaries/][color=#b48cff]Engine Boundaries[/color][/url], [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/performance/luajit-bytecode/][color=#b48cff]LuaJIT Bytecode[/color][/url], and [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/performance/luajit-traces/][color=#b48cff]Traces[/color][/url].[/color]

[center][size=4][b][url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/paid-for-with-blood/][color=#bfc1c9]Paid For With Blood[/color][/url][/b][/size][/center]

[color=#d4d4d8]Some rules exist because somebody already paid the debugging cost for you.[/color]

[color=#d4d4d8]Paid For With Blood documents real bugs, regressions, failed assumptions, performance cliffs, abandoned designs, and historical fixes, with direct links to the commits wherever possible.[/color]

[color=#d4d4d8]The point is not to pretend the current architecture was obvious from the beginning. The failures are part of the documentation.[/color]
