[center][size=4][b][url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/][color=#b48cff]THE COMPLETE COD3X FIELD MANUAL[/color][/url][/b][/size][/center]

[color=#d4d4d8]New to OpenMW Lua? Start with [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/zero-to-hero/][color=#7ee2a8]Zero to Hero[/color][/url].[/color]

[color=#d4d4d8]Already shipping mods? Jump directly into [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/reference/][color=#b48cff]Reference and Mental Maps[/color][/url], [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/good-designs/][color=#f2c66d]Good Designs[/color][/url], [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/practice/][color=#b48cff]Engineering Practice[/color][/url], [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/performance/][color=#ff9875]Performance[/color][/url], or [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/paid-for-with-blood/][color=#bfc1c9]Paid For With Blood[/color][/url].[/color]

[color=#d4d4d8]Need a specific small answer? Check the [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/cookbook/][color=#b48cff]Cookbook[/color][/url]. Need to understand why an API exists only somewhere? Read [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/getting-started/contexts/][color=#b48cff]Script Contexts[/color][/url]. Need to know why a rule sounds suspiciously specific? There is probably a corpse in [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/paid-for-with-blood/][color=#bfc1c9]Paid For With Blood[/color][/url].[/color]
[heading][center][color=#b48cff]Editor setup[/color][/center]
[/heading]

[color=#d4d4d8]Cod3x is not a normal OpenMW mod. Do not enable it in the OpenMW launcher.[/color]

[color=#d4d4d8]The repository contains Lua Language Server annotations, an OpenMW context plugin, documentation, and example projects. The tooling portion is loaded by your editor, not by OpenMW.[/color]

[color=#d4d4d8]You need an editor with LuaLS support. For VSCode and its forks, install the Lua Language Server extension.[/color]

[color=#d4d4d8]Download Cod3x manually and extract it somewhere stable.[/color]

[color=#d4d4d8]For example:[/color]

[code]C:/Modding/Tools/Cod3x/
/home/you/Modding/Tools/Cod3x/[/code]

[color=#d4d4d8]The folder contains the OpenMW annotations, context plugin, examples, and supporting metadata.[/color]

[color=#d4d4d8]Copy [font=Courier New].luarc.json[/font] from [font=Courier New]examples/openmw-mod/[/font] into the root of your Lua mod, then replace the Cod3x paths with the directory where you extracted it:[/color]

[code]{
  "runtime.version": "LuaJIT",
  "workspace.library": ["/absolute/path/to/Cod3x"],
  "runtime.plugin": "/absolute/path/to/Cod3x/omw_context_plugin.lua"
}[/code]

[color=#d4d4d8]The complete [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/zero-to-hero/setup-cod3x/][color=#b48cff]Set Up Cod3x walkthrough[/color][/url] shows how to configure LuaLS and prove that the context plugin is actually working.[/color]
[heading][center][color=#b48cff]Context-aware OpenMW diagnostics[/color][/center]
[/heading]

[color=#d4d4d8]OpenMW scripts do not all run with the same authority. Global, local, player, menu, and load scripts expose different parts of the engine. See [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/getting-started/contexts/][color=#b48cff]Script Contexts[/color][/url] for the full mental model.[/color]

[color=#d4d4d8]Tell Cod3x which context a source file belongs to:[/color]

[code]---@omw-context player[/code]

[color=#d4d4d8]Supported contexts include:[/color]

[list]
[*][font=Courier New]global[/font] — world-level script context[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]local[/font] — script attached to an in-world object[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]player[/font] — player-specific behavior, input, camera, and UI[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]menu[/font] — menu behavior and menu UI[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]load[/font] — content-loading scripts[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]runtime[/font] — shared code valid in normal runtime contexts[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]all[/font] — code valid in every OpenMW script context[/*]
[*][font=Courier New]none[/font] — code intentionally using no OpenMW APIs[/*]
[/list]
[color=#d4d4d8]Contexts can also be combined:[/color]

[code]---@omw-context global | player[/code]

[color=#d4d4d8]And narrowed locally when a runtime branch genuinely changes what the code is allowed to do:[/color]

[code]---@omw-context global | player
local core = require 'openmw.core'

---@omw-context-next player
local camera = require 'openmw.camera'

---@omw-context-begin player
local input = require 'openmw.input'
local ui = require 'openmw.ui'
---@omw-context-end[/code]

[color=#d4d4d8]These annotations are assertions for LuaLS. They do not change runtime permissions. Cod3x uses them to catch context mistakes before you boot the game. The deeper tooling explanation lives in [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/tooling/luals/][color=#83c7ff]Cod3x and LuaLS[/color][/url].[/color]
[heading][center][color=#b48cff]What the tooling gives you[/color][/center]
[/heading]

[list]
[*]Autocomplete for OpenMW Lua APIs[/*]
[*]Hover documentation directly in your editor[/*]
[*]Function parameters and return types[/*]
[*]Nilability information instead of mystery [font=Courier New]unknown[/font] values[/*]
[*]Context-aware diagnostics for OpenMW modules, interfaces, and selected members[/*]
[*]LuaLS-native models for OpenMW objects, records, constants, and enums[/*]
[*]Support for typing your own mod interfaces[/*]
[*]A maintained machine-readable companion to the human field manual[/*]
[/list]

[center][size=4][b][color=#b48cff]Make your editor understand OpenMW before OpenMW teaches you the hard way.[/color][/b][/size][/center]
[heading][center][color=#b48cff]Type your own interfaces[/color][/center]
[/heading]

[color=#d4d4d8]Public interfaces deserve explicit contracts. The [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/getting-started/events-and-interfaces/][color=#b48cff]Events and Interfaces[/color][/url] guide explains when an interface is the right communication boundary, while [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/reference/interfaces/][color=#b48cff]Built-In Interfaces[/color][/url] maps OpenMW's own interface surface.[/color]

[color=#d4d4d8]You can extend [font=Courier New]openmw.interfaces[/font] for your own project through a workspace-only LuaLS metadata file. OpenMW does not need to load it.[/color]

[code]---@meta

---@class openmw.interfaces
---@field MyMod? openmw.interfaces.MyMod

---@class openmw.interfaces.MyMod
---@field version string
---@field resolve fun(recordId: string): boolean[/code]

[color=#d4d4d8]Cod3x documents both the mechanism and the architecture around interfaces: ownership, communication boundaries, context, versioning, and when an event is the more appropriate tool.[/color]
[heading][center][color=#b48cff]Source, history, and receipts[/color][/center]
[/heading]

[color=#d4d4d8]Cod3x goes out of its way to link engineering advice back to real implementations. Read [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/provenance/][color=#b48cff]Provenance and Research Corpus[/color][/url] for where the material came from and how evidence is treated.[/color]

[color=#d4d4d8]Where useful, documentation links directly to:[/color]

[list]
[*]current H3 patterns and API documentation[/*]
[*]production implementations in S3maphore, SSS, T4rg3t5, and other DreamWeave projects[/*]
[*]OpenMW source and API behavior[/*]
[*]historical commits where designs changed[/*]
[*]failed experiments and subsequent fixes[/*]
[*]the current implementation after that history settled[/*]
[/list]
[color=#d4d4d8]The goal is to make the path from principle → implementation → history navigable instead of asking everyone to rediscover it independently.[/color]

[color=#d4d4d8]If you want to dig yourself, use the [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/tooling/source-diving/][color=#b48cff]Source-Diving Workflow[/color][/url]. If you want the receipts, browse the [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/reference/history-index/][color=#b48cff]Historical Evidence Index[/color][/url].[/color]

[center][size=4][b][color=#b48cff]So You Want to Code with AI?[/color][/b][/size][/center]

[color=#d4d4d8]Okay. Fine.[/color]

[color=#d4d4d8]With great power comes great responsibility.[/color]

[color=#d4d4d8]AI can dramatically increase how much code you are capable of producing. That means you should be [i]more[/i] responsible for understanding, testing, profiling, and reviewing what you ship — not less.[/color]

[color=#d4d4d8]If you are new to programming, use Cod3x to learn what the agent is doing instead of treating generated code as magic. Start with [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/zero-to-hero/][color=#7ee2a8]Zero to Hero[/color][/url], then let the manual teach you the concepts behind the code your agent writes.[/color]

[color=#d4d4d8]If you already know what you are doing, Cod3x gives an agent the same things that help a human engineer work responsibly: API contracts, project conventions, known-good patterns, source history, performance guidance, and receipts.[/color]

[center][size=4][b][color=#b48cff]If your laptop can code itself now, the least you can do is give the thing a couple pointers.[/color][/b][/size][/center]

[color=#d4d4d8]Read [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/tooling/so-you-want-to-code-with-ai/][color=#b48cff]So You Want To Code With AI?[/color][/url] for practical guidance on repository instructions, source-grounding, review, testing, validation, Git history, and making an agent prove its work instead of merely sounding confident.[/color]

[color=#d4d4d8]The standard does not go down because a machine typed the code.[/color]

[color=#d4d4d8][b]The standard goes up.[/b][/color]
[heading][center][color=#b48cff]Requirements[/color][/center]
[/heading]

[color=#d4d4d8]Cod3x currently targets OpenMW 0.52 and the LuaJIT-based scripting environment used by that release line. Older versions may expose different APIs, types, interfaces, and behavior.[/color]

[color=#d4d4d8]The field manual also documents deeper runtime and binding behavior where it is relevant to engineering and performance. If you want to understand where Lua-side optimization eventually stops being enough, read [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/performance/rubic0n/][color=#ff9875]When Lua-Side Optimization Stops Being Enough[/color][/url].[/color]

[heading][center][color=#b48cff]Provenance and shout outs[/color][/center]
[/heading]

[color=#d4d4d8]Cod3x did not appear in a vacuum.[/color]

[color=#d4d4d8]It carries forward years of OpenMW community knowledge, earlier annotation work, Yvan/Hebi's Effective OpenMW Lua project, production lessons from DreamWeave projects, upstream OpenMW implementation details, and ideas contributed or explored by many people across the community.[/color]

[color=#d4d4d8]Where a technique, implementation, or historical influence can be traced to a specific source, the field manual tries to preserve that provenance rather than quietly absorbing it. See [url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/provenance/][color=#b48cff]Provenance and Research Corpus[/color][/url].[/color]

AI media disclosure: Cod3x's new images are generated, but not by an image model - [url=https://github.com/DreamWeave-MP/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/tree/main/static/img/cod3x]just classic magick.[/url]

[heading][center][color=#b48cff]One last thing[/color][/center]
[/heading]

[center][color=#d4d4d8]Protected execution is appropriate when failure is genuinely expected and recoverable.[/color]

[color=#d4d4d8]It is not a substitute for correct program state, proper ownership, or understanding why something failed.[/color][/center]

[center][size=5][b][color=#b48cff]I will hunt you for using pcall.[/color][/b][/size][/center]

[center][url=https://dreamweave-mp.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/cod3x/docs/practice/error-handling/][color=#d4d4d8]Yes, there is an entire page explaining why.[/color][/url][/center]

